            <ArticleTitle>The Christensen prosthesis. A retrospective clinical study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>273-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The objective of this study was to determine if the Christensen temporomandibular joint prosthesis system in an effective alternative in treating patients with severe temporomandibular joint disorders. A total of 69 patients who were not responsive to either nonsurgical or prior surgical treatments were placed into one of three treatment groups depending on the following diagnoses: (1) placement of a glenoid fossa-eminence prosthesis with meniscus retention (22 patients, 40 joints); (2) placement of a glenoid fossa-eminence prosthesis without retention of the meniscus (26 patients, 49 joints); (3) total joint replacement (21 patients, 34 joints). Patients were evaluated immediately before surgery and at regular intervals after surgery for an average of 3.1 years. Success was measured as a significant improvement of function and decrease in pain as measured on a visual analogue scale, as well as improved incisor opening as measured with a Therabite Scale (Lorenz Surgical, Jacksonville, Fla.). Comparison of mean and average pre- and postsurgical values for all groups and criteria showed significant improvement. Results of this study indicate that the Christensen temporomandibular joint prosthesis system may offer a viable method for the treatment of severe temporomandibular joint disease with a high degree of success.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
